{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"8d40d0f3a72c125b","type":"text","text":"Easter egg hunt\n# Evaluating the effects of collaboration and competition in navigation tasks and spatial knowledge acquisition within virtual reality environments\n\nHaving a mapped out environment and having people collaborate to navigate around it\n\nThere are a specific number of items that participants need to collect in order to complete the task. They are located in different shops around the virtual shopping centre\n\nThe 9 items were distributed evenly in the three floors – 3 items on each floor – and were positioned to make participants cover all the locations where there were shops that participants would need to recall later. Three shops were placed on the ground and second floors each and the remaining four shops were placed on the first floor (see Fig. 5 for the shop labels used). Participants had no prior knowledge of their distribution and exact location.\n\nCOO = collaborative COM = Competitive SIN = Single\nParticipants in COO and COM groups would do the experiment either standing or sitting next to each other and would begin in the same location within the VE. Each participant was able to see the other participant’s location in the VE in the form of an avatar\n\nSimilar to the practice VE, items in SIN group would disappear once collected.\n\nThe time it took to collect each item was recorded by the system. After finishing the experiment, participants would then move to the second stage of the third phase: the completion of the memory test, which would require them to locate the shops and items on a map with the layout of the shopping mall (see Fig. 7). On this map, shops were represented as empty rectangles and items as diamonds or small squares. During the test, participants were once again given access to information sheet with the list of items and labels of shops.","x":-224,"y":-740,"width":504,"height":1020},
		{"id":"295c6daefa1ebdda","type":"text","text":"Tetris / building block game \n\n- Six blocks scattered throughout the room \n- Goal of the task is to assemble all the pieces in the correct order\n- The blocks need to be collected in a specific order\n- One person knows the order that they need to be collected in (numbered / coloured blocks)\n- One person knows the order they need to be built in \n- One person knows how the tower needs to be built\n- They cannot explicitly share their instruction sheet with one another \n\nThe blocks are scattered around the room, some close to one another. Only one block at a time can be picked up until they are placed. \n","x":-761,"y":-692,"width":381,"height":592},
		{"id":"f7f44e006c64b8e5","type":"text","text":"Whiteboard task \n\nThere are 3 whiteboards scattered around the room\n\n- Each participant needs to read what is on each whiteboard and perform the task that is written for them ","x":325,"y":-715,"width":455,"height":485},
		{"id":"f9fccee1a78cf46b","type":"text","text":"TO DO FOR EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN\n\n- Primary research questions\n\t- Navigation confidence, collision awareness, effects of virtuality, comparison with face-to-face\n\t\t- Navigation - Hypothesis define\n\t\t- Collision awareness - find definition, and link\n\t\t- Virtuality - Find papers that have compared something similar - Groenbaek has papers investigating blended realities, invesitgate if relevant\n\t\t- Face-to-Face - Proxemic behaviour probably the same, what about in terms of navigation? \n\t\t\t- What do we expect to see - find citation\n\t\t\t- Collision awareness - what distances are common, is there anything that has exact distances?\n- Defining a task\n\t- ","x":-542,"y":-1154,"width":682,"height":354}
	],
	"edges":[]
